Q2 — Incremental Load: Only Process New CDC Records Since Last Run
------------------------------------------------------------------

Your pipeline runs daily. On 2024-03-03 it failed (R03 in
pipeline_audit). On 2024-03-04 it re-runs and needs to pick up
ALL records it missed — not just today's, but also Mar 03's.

Write a query that:
1. Finds the last successful load timestamp from pipeline_audit
   for the orders_cdc pipeline
2. Returns all CDC records with updated_at > that timestamp
3. Does NOT hardcode the date — must read from pipeline_audit


QUERY:
------
WITH last_success AS (
    SELECT MAX(last_loaded_at) AS last_run_at
    FROM pipeline_audit
    WHERE pipeline_name = 'orders_cdc'
      AND status = 'success'
)
SELECT
    cdc.*
FROM orders_cdc cdc
CROSS JOIN last_success ls
WHERE cdc.updated_at > ls.last_run_at;


TRAP:
-----
If you use MAX(run_date) instead of MAX(last_loaded_at) you get
the wrong cutoff. run_date is the calendar date of the pipeline
execution. last_loaded_at is the actual timestamp of the last
record processed. These differ when pipeline runs at 11pm but
records arrive at midnight — you would miss the gap.

Also: if ALL runs have failed (last_success returns NULL),
the WHERE clause becomes updated_at > NULL which returns zero
rows silently. Always handle the NULL case:

    WHERE cdc.updated_at > COALESCE(ls.last_run_at, '1900-01-01')

EXPECTED OUTPUT:
All CDC records with updated_at > 2024-03-02 23:00:00
→ Only O005 (2024-03-03 09:00:00) qualifies from clean data.


