Q3 — SCD Type 2 Merge: Apply Snapshot Changes to Dimension Table
-----------------------------------------------------------------

You have dim_customers in its Mar 01 state. The Mar 02 snapshot
arrived. Write a query that produces the CORRECT updated state of
dim_customers after applying Mar 02 changes.

Rules for SCD Type 2:
- If a customer's tracked attributes changed → close the old row
  (set valid_to = new snapshot_date - 1 day, is_current = false)
  and INSERT a new row (valid_from = snapshot_date, valid_to = NULL,
  is_current = true)
- If nothing changed → do nothing (do not insert a duplicate row)
- Tracked attributes: plan, credit_limit (name and city are not tracked)


QUERY:
------
-- Step 1: Identify changed customers
WITH mar02 AS (
    SELECT *
    FROM customers_snapshot
    WHERE snapshot_date = '2024-03-02'
),
changed AS (
    SELECT
        m.customer_id,
        m.name,
        m.city,
        m.plan        AS new_plan,
        m.credit_limit AS new_credit_limit,
        d.plan        AS old_plan,
        d.credit_limit AS old_credit_limit,
        d.sk
    FROM mar02 m
    JOIN dim_customers d
        ON m.customer_id = d.customer_id
        AND d.is_current = true
    WHERE m.plan != d.plan
       OR m.credit_limit != d.credit_limit
),
-- Step 2: Rows to close (existing current rows that changed)
rows_to_close AS (
    SELECT sk FROM changed
),
-- Step 3: New rows to insert for changed customers
new_rows AS (
    SELECT
        customer_id,
        name,
        city,
        new_plan        AS plan,
        new_credit_limit AS credit_limit,
        '2024-03-02'   AS valid_from,
        NULL            AS valid_to,
        true            AS is_current
    FROM changed
)
-- Final: show what dim_customers looks like after merge
SELECT
    d.sk,
    d.customer_id,
    d.name,
    d.city,
    d.plan,
    d.credit_limit,
    d.valid_from,
    CASE WHEN d.sk IN (SELECT sk FROM rows_to_close)
         THEN '2024-03-01' ELSE d.valid_to END AS valid_to,
    CASE WHEN d.sk IN (SELECT sk FROM rows_to_close)
         THEN false ELSE d.is_current END AS is_current
FROM dim_customers d

UNION ALL

SELECT
    NULL AS sk,  -- new SK assigned by sequence in real pipeline
    customer_id,
    name,
    city,
    plan,
    credit_limit,
    valid_from,
    valid_to,
    is_current
FROM new_rows
ORDER BY customer_id, valid_from;


TRAP:
-----
Beginners close the old row and insert the new one in the same
UPDATE + INSERT. But if you forget the is_current = true filter
in the JOIN you will match ALL historical rows for a customer
(not just the current one) and produce multiple new rows per
customer. Always filter is_current = true before joining.

EXPECTED OUTPUT AFTER MERGE:
| sk   | customer_id | plan     | credit_limit | valid_from | valid_to   | is_current |
|------|-------------|----------|--------------|------------|------------|------------|
| 1    | C01         | gold     | 50000        | 2024-03-01 | NULL       | true       |
| 2    | C02         | silver   | 30000        | 2024-03-01 | 2024-03-01 | false      |
| NULL | C02         | gold     | 40000        | 2024-03-02 | NULL       | true       |
| 3    | C03         | bronze   | 15000        | 2024-03-01 | NULL       | true       |

C01 unchanged on Mar 02 → no new row.
C02 changed plan+credit → old row closed, new row inserted.
C03 unchanged → no new row.


