Q6 — Partitioning Awareness: Rewrite a Query to Avoid Full Scan
----------------------------------------------------------------

Your orders_cdc table in the warehouse is PARTITIONED by
DATE(updated_at). The following query is used in a daily job
but it does a full table scan because the WHERE clause prevents
partition pruning:

-- SLOW VERSION (runs on 500M row table, full scan):
SELECT order_id, status, amount
FROM orders_cdc
WHERE YEAR(updated_at) = 2024
  AND MONTH(updated_at) = 3;

Rewrite it to enable partition pruning. Then explain in a comment
why the original fails and yours works.


QUERY:
------
-- FAST VERSION (partition pruning enabled):
SELECT order_id, status, amount
FROM orders_cdc
WHERE updated_at >= '2024-03-01 00:00:00'
  AND updated_at <  '2024-04-01 00:00:00';

-- WHY THE ORIGINAL IS SLOW:
-- YEAR(updated_at) and MONTH(updated_at) wrap the partition key
-- in a function. The query planner cannot resolve which partitions
-- to skip without evaluating the function on every row.
-- This forces a full table scan even though the table is partitioned.

-- WHY THE REWRITE IS FAST:
-- updated_at >= '2024-03-01' AND updated_at < '2024-04-01' uses
-- the raw partition key directly in a range comparison.
-- The planner can immediately identify which partitions fall in
-- this range and skip all others. On a 500M row table partitioned
-- daily this difference is scanning 31 partitions vs all 500+.

-- SECOND TRAP — timezone:
-- If the warehouse stores updated_at in UTC but your app sends
-- timestamps in IST (UTC+5:30), '2024-03-01 00:00:00' in your
-- query is actually '2024-02-29 18:30:00' UTC. You will miss
-- the first 5.5 hours of Mar 01 data. Always confirm timezone
-- convention before writing range predicates on timestamp columns.


