Q8 — Slowly Changing Dimension Type 1 vs Type 2 Decision
----------------------------------------------------------

Your customers_snapshot table shows that C01 changed plan from
gold → platinum between Mar 02 and Mar 03.

The business comes to you with two requirements:

Requirement A (Finance team):
"Show me what plan each customer was on when each order was placed.
 Historical accuracy matters — if C01 placed an order on Mar 01
 under gold plan, show gold, even if they are now platinum."

Requirement B (Marketing team):
"Show me each customer's current plan. We only care about today."

Write two separate queries demonstrating why Requirement A needs
SCD Type 2 and Requirement B only needs SCD Type 1.
Use the orders_cdc and customers_snapshot tables.


QUERY FOR REQUIREMENT A (SCD Type 2 — point-in-time lookup):
-------------------------------------------------------------
-- After applying Q3 merge logic, dim_customers has history.
-- Join orders to the dimension using the order date falling
-- within the valid_from / valid_to range of the customer record.

SELECT
    o.order_id,
    o.customer_id,
    o.amount,
    o.status,
    DATE(o.updated_at)   AS order_date,
    d.plan               AS plan_at_time_of_order,
    d.credit_limit       AS credit_limit_at_time_of_order
FROM (
    -- Use Q1 deduplicated orders
    SELECT DISTINCT ON (order_id) *
    FROM orders_cdc
    ORDER BY order_id, updated_at DESC
) o
JOIN dim_customers d
    ON o.customer_id = d.customer_id
    AND DATE(o.updated_at) >= d.valid_from
    AND (d.valid_to IS NULL OR DATE(o.updated_at) <= d.valid_to);


QUERY FOR REQUIREMENT B (SCD Type 1 — current state only):
----------------------------------------------------------
-- Just take the latest snapshot. No history needed.

SELECT
    c.customer_id,
    c.name,
    c.plan               AS current_plan,
    c.credit_limit       AS current_credit_limit
FROM customers_snapshot c
WHERE c.snapshot_date = (SELECT MAX(snapshot_date)
                         FROM customers_snapshot);


TRAP:
-----
Requirement A is where most engineers make the mistake of joining
on customer_id alone without the date range condition. This gives
every order the customer's CURRENT plan, not their plan at the
time of the order. For financial reporting this is a compliance
violation — you are reporting incorrect historical data.

The valid_to IS NULL condition handles current (open-ended) records.
If you write valid_to IS NULL OR valid_to >= order_date you get
the same result but the IS NULL check must come first or the
planner may not use the index on valid_to efficiently.


