Q9 — Idempotent Pipeline: Safe to Re-run Without Duplicates
------------------------------------------------------------

Your pipeline loads the deduplicated orders into a clean
orders_clean table. It failed midway on Mar 03 and loaded
only 2 of 5 rows before crashing. On re-run it must not
duplicate those 2 rows.

orders_clean (current state after partial load)
| order_id | customer_id | amount  | status    | updated_at          |
|----------|-------------|---------|-----------|---------------------|
| O001     | C01         | 1200.00 | returned  | 2024-03-02 13:00:00 |
| O002     | C01         | 850.00  | cancelled | 2024-03-01 15:00:00 |

Write an idempotent MERGE/UPSERT query that loads the full Q1
output into orders_clean safely — inserting new rows and updating
existing ones if the status or updated_at changed.


QUERY:
------
-- MySQL / BigQuery style (INSERT ... ON DUPLICATE KEY UPDATE)
INSERT INTO orders_clean
    (order_id, customer_id, amount, status, updated_at)
SELECT
    order_id, customer_id, amount, status, updated_at
FROM (
    -- Q1 deduplicated result inline
    WITH deduped AS (
        SELECT DISTINCT order_id, customer_id, amount, status, updated_at, op
        FROM orders_cdc
    ),
    latest AS (
        SELECT *,
               ROW_NUMBER() OVER (
                   PARTITION BY order_id ORDER BY updated_at DESC
               ) AS rn
        FROM deduped
    )
    SELECT order_id, customer_id, amount, status, updated_at
    FROM latest
    WHERE rn = 1 AND op != 'DELETE'
) src
ON DUPLICATE KEY UPDATE
    status     = VALUES(status),
    updated_at = VALUES(updated_at),
    customer_id = VALUES(customer_id),
    amount     = VALUES(amount);

-- PostgreSQL style (INSERT ... ON CONFLICT DO UPDATE):
/*
INSERT INTO orders_clean
    (order_id, customer_id, amount, status, updated_at)
SELECT order_id, customer_id, amount, status, updated_at
FROM deduplicated_source
ON CONFLICT (order_id) DO UPDATE SET
    status     = EXCLUDED.status,
    updated_at = EXCLUDED.updated_at
WHERE orders_clean.updated_at < EXCLUDED.updated_at;
*/


TRAP:
-----
The PostgreSQL version has a critical WHERE clause:
    WHERE orders_clean.updated_at < EXCLUDED.updated_at

Without this, a re-run could DOWNGRADE an order — if the pipeline
re-processes older CDC records and the ON CONFLICT fires, it would
overwrite a 'returned' status (from Mar 02) with a 'pending' status
(from Mar 01). The WHERE ensures you only update if the incoming
row is NEWER than what is already stored. This is called
"last-write-wins with timestamp guard" and is essential in any
idempotent CDC pipeline.


